on without  saying a word but while 343 does this  from time to time i found there not to  be as many action scenes this time  around only a handful of them were  actually wide shots as most of them were  all really up close this conveyed to me  that we were not experiencing an action  movie but more a somber deeply personal  story and that's what infinite is really  about yes there was a lot of action and  the threat of humanity being at stake is  still very relevant again but it never  felt like that plot was at center stage  more of just something to break up the  actual story of infinite which was  chief's relationship with cortana the  weapon and the pilot despite the chief  constantly saying that cortana needs to  be deleted and trying to back up his  statements by referencing all the past  atrocities she committed we all know the  chief did not want to do that even  during the game we hear him mention how  everything that happened was his fault  and how he wished he could have reasoned  with her except we know that cortana was  never going to leave her position of  power due to their conversation in halo  5. as she went as far as trapping blue  team into kryptum so that they couldn't  interfere with her work she never wanted  to kill them as they are family to her  but she also didn't want them to ruin  her plans however while initially he  feels remorseful he starts to evolve  over the course of the campaign and  learned to accept his fate and also  learn to accept those he's grown close  to both the pilot and the weapon do like  the chief there is no doubt about that  but chief's actions throughout the game  soured their relationships quite a bit  the pilot rescued the chief back in the  beginning of the game and was super  excited to find him not because he was  going to help wipe out the banish but  that's so he could have a safe escort  home we discovered that the pilot wasn't  actually a pilot or a marine he stole  the pelican that he pilots he explains  that he was scared and that he didn't  want to die so he stole the pelican and  has been hiding in it for months when we  make it to zeta halo the pilot thinks  the chief is going there to retrieve a  physical weapon that he can use to  defeat the banished as they try to  escape but the master chief has other  plans as he has no intention of actually  leaving zeta halo until the banished are  gone the chief never lets the pilot know  this and is being dragged along thinking  that after this he'll go home like okay  the chief is here we can go home oh wait  he wants a weapon alright well after the  weapon then we can go home oh the  banister trying to rebuild these spires  to repair the ring okay i get that but  after that then we can go home it's not  until they crash land when the pilot  finally blows up on him about it his  mood does slightly change as he  discovers numerous frigates nearby that  may have slip space drives that they can  use but the master chief says his  mission is more important but after it's  done they'll go search for one except  it's too late as when he gets back from  his mission all the frigates were  scrapped and the tech was destroyed he  once again blows up on the chief and  starts throwing all the debris around as  all he wants to do is go home he doesn't  care about this war anymore as humanity  has already lost he just wants to go  home and not be caught in the crossfire  the chief then sits down next to him and  explained that everyone makes mistakes  and that making mistakes is what means  to be human to the pilot the chief may  as well be a divine being there's no way  a mere human can stop the covenant army  and annihilate the flood it has to be  the work of a god but the master chief  really is just a human and 343 has  started taking the chief down this path  of realism and it's one that bungie  never really touched on before master  chief in the original trilogy had very  little faults and was a master of combat  everything 